bevy-horizon: flatten larger ifs before Volta, transcendentals too (NAK_DEBUG=pselx)

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 953fa75a, after 0001-0030. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

psel (patch 0026) turns an if whose sides hold up to 8 instructions into selects, so it pays no
SSY, BRA and SYNC, but nir_opt_peephole_select leaves any if with a transcendental (rsq, sqrt,
log2, exp2), which NIR counts as expensive. pselx raises the limit to 24 and lets those in. It
flattens only ALU: speculated loads (indirect_load_ok) changed none of Skate 3's world shaders,
so they stay out. Off by default, NAK_DEBUG=pselx turns it on; the flags key NVK's shader cache.

Skate 3's main-pass world shaders on the host (sk8 findings 62 §19), with the Switch's default
flags, instructions and registers:

  shader                               default      pselx
  opaque, MaloofMoneyCup's 0x77        1,849, 48    1,763, 48
  opaque, all features (Downtown)      1,976, 56    1,938, 48 (40 warps, not 36)
  opaque, features 0x57                1,760, 48    1,676, 48
  water                                2,710, 120   2,657, 120

NAK's cycle estimate falls 2-4% for three of them and rises 4% for the second, but it predicted
neither psel and tlat's measured saving nor ubra's measured loss, so only the console can say.

